1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,018
Double-click the element to open its
scene.

2
00:00:04,018 --> 00:00:09,500
Every element scene has the same simple
shape, so let's look at it.

3
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:19,919
At the top, the element's root, with our
script on it.

4
00:00:19,919 --> 00:00:21,638
Under it, one Canvas.

5
00:00:21,638 --> 00:00:25,321
And under the Canvas, one object called
Root.

6
00:00:25,321 --> 00:00:31,786
Root is the important one: it's what moves
when the screen animates in and out.

7
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:45,047
So there's one rule: everything you build
goes under Root.

8
00:00:45,047 --> 00:00:46,929
Nothing sits next to it.

9
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:59,672
Right-click Root and add an image for the
background.

10
00:00:59,672 --> 00:01:02,034
Stretch it to fill the screen.

11
00:01:02,034 --> 00:01:03,057
Then a title.

12
00:01:03,057 --> 00:01:09,197
Use normal anchors, exactly like any Unity
UI: the canvas scaler takes care of

13
00:01:09,197 --> 00:01:10,929
different phone sizes.

14
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:49,713
Now a Play button, and a Store button
under it.

15
00:01:49,713 --> 00:01:54,464
We'll make the Store button work later in
the series.

16
00:01:54,464 --> 00:01:59,214
Here it is in the Game view, on a phone-
sized screen.

17
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:32,357
Everything lives under Root.

18
00:02:32,357 --> 00:02:35,077
That's what keeps the animations working.

19
00:02:35,077 --> 00:02:36,071
Save the scene.

20
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:52,689
Our first screen is built.

21
00:02:52,689 --> 00:02:55,214
Next, we give it an animation.
